# Introduction to Cloud Computing

Cloud computing is a model of delivering computing resources, infrastructure, platforms, and software services through a network, usually the internet. Instead of an organization purchasing, installing, operating, and maintaining every physical server, storage device, networking component, database system, and software platform on its own premises, it can obtain these resources from a cloud provider whenever they are required. Cloud computing has fundamentally changed the way organizations think about information technology because computing resources can now be treated as services that can be provisioned, configured, scaled, monitored, and managed through web interfaces, application programming interfaces, automation tools, and code. At its most fundamental level, cloud computing does not mean that physical computers have disappeared. Every cloud service ultimately runs on physical infrastructure located in data centers. These data centers contain large numbers of physical servers, storage devices, networking equipment, power systems, cooling systems, security systems, and other infrastructure components. The major difference between traditional computing and cloud computing is the way these physical resources are organized, managed, abstracted, and delivered to customers. In a traditional infrastructure environment, an organization may directly own and operate physical servers and related equipment. In a cloud environment, a cloud provider owns and operates large-scale infrastructure and provides customers with access to logical or virtual computing resources. The development of cloud computing is closely connected to the historical evolution of computing infrastructure. Early computing was dominated by mainframes, where a large centralized computer provided computing capabilities to multiple users. Computing later became more distributed with the development of personal computers. Client-server computing then became popular, allowing applications to divide responsibilities between client machines and centralized servers. As organizations grew, they began building large enterprise data centers containing physical servers, storage systems, networking equipment, and supporting infrastructure. These environments provided organizations with direct control over their computing systems, but they were expensive and complicated to operate. Traditional data center infrastructure required organizations to predict their future computing requirements. If a company expected its application to receive a large number of users, it had to purchase sufficient servers, storage, and networking capacity before the demand actually occurred. This process is known as capacity planning. Capacity planning is difficult because future demand is uncertain. If an organization purchases too much infrastructure, expensive resources may remain underutilized. If it purchases too little infrastructure, applications may become overloaded when demand increases. Purchasing and installing new physical hardware can also require significant time because the organization may need to select vendors, purchase equipment, wait for delivery, install hardware, configure networks, and deploy software. Cloud computing addresses many of these challenges by allowing organizations to provision computing resources more dynamically. Instead of purchasing a physical server and waiting for it to be delivered and installed, a user may be able to create a virtual machine through a cloud management interface or an API. Instead of purchasing storage hardware for future requirements, the organization can allocate storage resources according to current requirements. This ability to provision infrastructure when required is one of the fundamental characteristics of cloud computing. One useful way to understand cloud computing is to compare it with electricity. Most individuals and organizations do not build their own power plants to generate electricity. Instead, they consume electricity from an infrastructure provider and pay according to a particular pricing model. Cloud computing applies a similar concept to computing resources. Organizations do not necessarily need to build and operate every component of their own computing infrastructure. They can consume computing resources provided by specialized organizations that operate infrastructure at a massive scale. Cloud computing offers several major characteristics. One of these characteristics is on-demand self-service. Users can provision computing resources when required without necessarily interacting with the physical infrastructure team responsible for installing servers. Another characteristic is broad network access, which means cloud resources can be accessed through networks using computers, mobile devices, applications, and other systems. Cloud computing also relies heavily on resource pooling, where providers maintain large pools of computing resources that can be allocated to customers according to their requirements. Resource pooling is important because cloud providers operate infrastructure at a massive scale. A provider may have large amounts of processing capacity, memory, storage, and networking resources distributed across multiple data centers. Instead of every customer owning dedicated physical hardware for every workload, cloud platforms can allocate resources dynamically using technologies such as virtualization, containerization, and software-defined infrastructure. This allows providers to achieve high levels of infrastructure utilization. Multi-tenancy is closely related to resource pooling. Multi-tenancy refers to an architecture in which multiple customers, organizations, or users can use shared underlying infrastructure while remaining logically isolated from one another. For example, different organizations may use virtual machines running on the same underlying physical infrastructure without being able to access each other's applications or data. Cloud providers use different isolation mechanisms involving virtualization, networking, identity management, access control, and security technologies to maintain separation between customers. Virtualization is one of the most important technologies associated with the development of modern cloud computing. Before virtualization became widely adopted, a physical server often ran a single operating system and a limited number of applications. This could result in inefficient resource utilization because many physical servers were not using their full processing and memory capacity. Virtualization introduced the ability to create multiple virtual machines on a single physical server. A virtual machine behaves like a logical computer. It can have its own operating system, applications, allocated CPU resources, memory, storage, and network configuration. A software component called a hypervisor helps manage the relationship between the physical hardware and the virtual machines. The hypervisor allows multiple isolated operating environments to share the resources of a physical server. Virtualization made it possible to use physical infrastructure more efficiently and created an important foundation for Infrastructure as a Service and cloud computing. Cloud computing also introduced the concept of scalability as a central infrastructure principle. Scalability is the ability of a system to increase its capacity when demand increases. There are two major approaches to scaling: vertical scaling and horizontal scaling. Vertical scaling means increasing the resources of an existing machine. For example, a virtual machine might be upgraded from four CPU cores and eight gigabytes of memory to sixteen CPU cores and sixty-four gigabytes of memory. Vertical scaling is relatively straightforward but has practical limits because a single machine cannot be increased indefinitely. Horizontal scaling means increasing system capacity by adding additional machines or resources. For example, instead of upgrading a single application server, an organization may deploy multiple application servers behind a load balancer. The load balancer distributes incoming traffic across the available servers. Horizontal scaling is particularly important in modern cloud infrastructure because it can allow systems to increase capacity by adding more independent resources. Elasticity is related to scalability but represents a more dynamic concept. Scalability means that a system is capable of increasing capacity. Elasticity means that the infrastructure can increase and decrease capacity according to changing demand. For example, an application might use two servers during periods of low traffic, ten servers during normal business hours, and one hundred servers during a major sale or event. When demand decreases, the infrastructure can reduce the number of active resources. This helps organizations match infrastructure capacity with actual workload requirements. Auto scaling is a technology and operational approach associated with elasticity. Cloud platforms can monitor metrics such as CPU utilization, memory consumption, network traffic, request rates, or custom application metrics. Based on predefined policies, the platform can add or remove computing resources. For example, if CPU utilization remains above a certain threshold for a specific period, additional application instances may be created. When utilization decreases, unnecessary instances may be removed. Cloud computing is attractive to organizations for several reasons. One important reason is speed. Infrastructure can often be provisioned much faster than purchasing and installing physical hardware. Another reason is scalability, which allows organizations to increase infrastructure capacity as applications grow. Elasticity allows infrastructure to adapt to changing workloads. Cloud providers also operate infrastructure in multiple geographic locations, allowing organizations to deploy applications closer to users around the world. Global infrastructure is another important concept in cloud computing. Large cloud providers operate infrastructure in different geographic regions. A cloud region generally represents a geographic area containing cloud infrastructure. Within a region, infrastructure may be divided into multiple isolated locations known as availability zones. Availability zones are designed to reduce the impact of localized infrastructure failures. An application deployed entirely on a single server may experience a complete outage if that server fails. Similarly, an application deployed in only one physical location may be affected by a failure in that location. Cloud infrastructure allows architects to design systems across multiple servers, availability zones, and regions. This supports high availability, fault tolerance, and disaster recovery. High availability refers to the ability of a system to remain operational for a large percentage of time. High availability architectures typically reduce single points of failure by introducing redundancy. For example, instead of using one application server, a system may use multiple application servers. Instead of using one database instance, a system may use replication and standby resources. Load balancers can distribute traffic between healthy resources and redirect traffic away from failed resources. Reliability is closely related to availability but focuses more broadly on the ability of a system to perform correctly over time. Cloud infrastructure engineers must consider many potential sources of failure, including hardware failures, software failures, network failures, human errors, configuration mistakes, security incidents, and unexpected workload patterns. Modern cloud architecture often follows the principle of designing for failure. Instead of assuming that infrastructure components will never fail, engineers design systems that can detect failures, isolate failures, and recover from failures. Cloud computing provides infrastructure through different service models. Infrastructure as a Service, commonly called IaaS, provides foundational infrastructure resources such as virtual machines, storage, and networking. The customer generally manages a larger portion of the operating environment, including the operating system and applications. IaaS provides flexibility and control but also requires the customer to manage more infrastructure responsibilities. Platform as a Service, or PaaS, provides managed application platforms and runtime environments. In this model, developers can focus more on developing and deploying applications while the cloud provider manages more of the underlying infrastructure. Software as a Service, or SaaS, provides complete software applications that users can access directly. The provider manages most of the underlying infrastructure and application environment. Function as a Service, often called FaaS or serverless computing, provides an even higher level of infrastructure abstraction. Developers create functions that execute in response to particular events. The cloud platform manages much of the infrastructure required to execute those functions. Serverless computing does not mean that servers do not exist. It means that developers do not directly manage many of the server-level infrastructure responsibilities. Cloud computing can also be organized using different deployment models. A public cloud provides infrastructure services operated by a cloud provider. A private cloud provides infrastructure dedicated to a single organization. A hybrid cloud combines private infrastructure with public cloud services. A multi-cloud strategy involves using services from more than one cloud provider. Organizations select deployment strategies based on technical requirements, security requirements, regulatory requirements, existing infrastructure, cost considerations, and business objectives. Cloud infrastructure consists of several major categories of services. Compute infrastructure provides processing capacity for applications. Examples include virtual machines, containers, and serverless functions. Storage infrastructure provides mechanisms for storing data. Common storage models include object storage, block storage, and file storage. Object storage stores information as objects and is commonly used for files, media, backups, logs, and large amounts of unstructured data. Block storage divides storage into blocks and is often used as virtual disks attached to compute instances. File storage provides a hierarchical file system that can be accessed by multiple systems depending on the storage architecture. Networking is another fundamental component of cloud infrastructure. Cloud platforms provide virtual networks that logically organize computing resources. These networks may include subnets, routing configurations, security controls, internet gateways, private connections, and load balancers. Cloud networking allows organizations to define how applications and services communicate with each other and with external users. Databases are also major components of cloud infrastructure. Cloud providers may offer managed relational databases, NoSQL databases, caching services, data warehouses, and other data platforms. Managed database services can reduce some operational responsibilities by providing features such as automated backups, maintenance, monitoring, and failover mechanisms. Security is one of the most important areas of cloud computing. Cloud infrastructure must protect identities, applications, networks, data, and computing resources. Important cloud security concepts include authentication, authorization, identity management, access control, least privilege, network security, encryption, secrets management, logging, monitoring, and incident response. Authentication determines who or what is attempting to access a system. Authorization determines what an authenticated user or system is allowed to do. Identity and Access Management systems are used to manage users, groups, roles, policies, and permissions. The principle of least privilege states that users and systems should receive only the permissions necessary to perform their intended tasks. Encryption is another important security mechanism. Encryption at rest protects stored data, while encryption in transit protects data as it travels through networks. Secrets management systems are used to securely manage sensitive information such as passwords, API keys, certificates, and credentials. Cloud security is commonly explained using the shared responsibility model. Cloud providers are responsible for operating and securing parts of the underlying infrastructure, such as physical data centers and core infrastructure services. Customers remain responsible for many aspects of their own environments, including user permissions, application configuration, data protection, and security policies. The exact distribution of responsibility changes depending on the service model. Cloud economics is another important concept. Traditional infrastructure often involves capital expenditure, or CAPEX. Organizations may need to make large upfront investments in physical servers, storage, networking equipment, and data center facilities. Cloud services commonly use consumption-based or operational expenditure models, although pricing structures can vary significantly. Cloud users may be charged based on factors such as compute time, storage capacity, data transfer, database usage, requests, or service-specific metrics. Cloud computing can reduce the need for certain upfront infrastructure investments, but it does not automatically guarantee lower costs. Poorly configured infrastructure, unused resources, unnecessary data transfer, excessive storage, and inefficient architectures can produce significant cloud expenses. Cloud cost management has therefore become an important area of cloud engineering. Organizations must monitor resource utilization, understand pricing models, remove unused infrastructure, select appropriate resource sizes, automate shutdowns for temporary environments, and design architectures that balance performance, availability, and cost. Cloud management can be performed through cloud consoles. A cloud console is generally a web-based management interface accessed through a browser. Users can create and configure resources such as virtual machines, storage systems, networks, databases, and identity configurations. Cloud consoles are useful for learning, experimentation, visualization, and direct management. Large-scale cloud environments increasingly rely on programmatic management. Cloud providers expose Application Programming Interfaces, or APIs, that allow software to communicate with cloud services. An API can be used to create, modify, monitor, and delete infrastructure resources programmatically. For example, instead of manually opening a cloud console and clicking a button to create a server, an application or automation tool can send an API request to the cloud platform. The platform processes the request and provisions the required infrastructure. This capability is fundamental to automation. Infrastructure as Code is the practice of managing infrastructure using code and configuration files. Instead of manually creating infrastructure resources one at a time, engineers can define the desired infrastructure in files. Automation tools can then use these definitions to create and configure infrastructure. Infrastructure as Code provides several important advantages. Infrastructure can be reproduced consistently, configuration changes can be tracked using version control systems, teams can collaborate on infrastructure definitions, and environments can be created automatically. Infrastructure as Code also reduces the risk of configuration differences caused by manual processes. Cloud-native computing is a broader approach to designing applications specifically for modern cloud environments. Cloud-native applications are typically designed with automation, scalability, distributed systems, containerization, continuous deployment, and observability in mind. Cloud-native architecture recognizes that modern applications often run across multiple servers and services rather than on one large machine. A modern cloud architecture may contain multiple layers. Users connect through the internet and access an application through services such as DNS and content delivery networks. Traffic may then reach a load balancer. The load balancer distributes requests across multiple application servers or containers. Applications may use caching systems to improve performance and databases to store persistent data. Supporting infrastructure provides monitoring, logging, identity management, encryption, auto scaling, backups, and disaster recovery. The move toward cloud computing also changed the role of infrastructure engineers. Traditional infrastructure management focused heavily on maintaining physical systems. Cloud infrastructure engineering requires knowledge of operating systems, networking, virtualization, security, automation, distributed systems, containers, Infrastructure as Code, monitoring, and reliability engineering. Cloud infrastructure is therefore not simply a collection of remote servers. It is a large ecosystem of interconnected technologies that provide computing capabilities through abstraction and automation. The underlying physical infrastructure remains essential, but cloud platforms provide logical layers that make computing resources easier to provision, manage, scale, and integrate. The most important foundational idea from this topic is that cloud computing represents a shift in how computing resources are consumed. Organizations move from treating infrastructure primarily as physical assets that must be purchased and maintained toward treating infrastructure as programmable services. Compute, storage, networking, databases, and other infrastructure components can increasingly be created and controlled through software. Understanding this foundation is essential before progressing to advanced cloud infrastructure topics. Linux provides the operating system knowledge required to manage cloud servers. Networking explains how cloud resources communicate. Virtualization explains how physical resources are abstracted. Storage explains how data is managed. Security protects cloud environments. Containers package applications. Kubernetes orchestrates containers. Infrastructure as Code automates infrastructure. DevOps and CI/CD automate software delivery. Monitoring and observability provide visibility into complex systems. Distributed systems, high availability, fault tolerance, and disaster recovery make applications more resilient. Cloud computing brings all of these areas together into a modern approach for building and operating digital systems. Learning cloud infrastructure therefore requires understanding both individual technologies and how those technologies interact to create secure, scalable, automated, reliable, and globally distributed computing environments.
